#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "657bd7d3-dd1c-5d1e-a2e2-e5779123f72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407284} {:answer/id #uuid "784f60d8-744f-5a20-8590-4309ac0a7fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac4f900-28cf-5d47-aca1-6ede50a776f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407287} {:answer/id #uuid "c63263fe-1e9f-59ea-a5b6-858826246c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407289} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc6305c-bf3a-5c13-af4f-f56b112873c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "784f60d8-744f-5a20-8590-4309ac0a7fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e76b06d-2177-5f41-ae22-04ed4efb0ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed965e5b-f0df-5c0f-9c7c-0cb7a433c067" :question/math-academy-id "q-86866" :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baba48d0-d9fb-5842-84e6-6919f8e36cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407328} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd0836e-e6f4-5b58-bfb5-c8dec7e9a94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407330} {:answer/id #uuid "f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407331} {:answer/id #uuid "f67abfd4-642f-5614-876f-390eb3d1595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407332} {:answer/id #uuid "fe963c24-be42-5cfe-a77e-8b918c0ffa49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c15890f-719b-5cd8-8053-341247002966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74214" :question/problem "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "368e8346-dba0-5eab-bd72-0c8e01860bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407324} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5b8efe-00bb-54bf-84b6-0c9423e627f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407325} {:answer/id #uuid "70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407326} {:answer/id #uuid "99103a99-9e82-5c24-8f3c-8db058da3029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407327} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6b9cf4-8a14-5d9c-8580-90b1f71a942c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407326} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1cab38-e87e-597f-87e0-47535946af67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35574" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we also have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da861c8-066a-5c61-b09e-b19fe4847a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407577} {:answer/id #uuid "55d4b454-b90c-53f1-bb03-1433437d6223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407580} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2e08a4-12e2-56a6-afe0-aaec4835514d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407585} {:answer/id #uuid "9b88c49e-083d-575c-8a5a-f1c566cae7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407588} {:answer/id #uuid "b06266a6-9eff-553d-bee6-4acee85a7d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da861c8-066a-5c61-b09e-b19fe4847a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407577} :answer-field/id #uuid "219bccf4-7364-5184-b837-9a47fc6fccc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db5f04ef-3cc1-58de-87ac-cce7945d8e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-183355" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\in Z.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\in Z.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. If $x\\in Z$ then $-x\\in Z$, and if $-x\\in Z$ then $x\\in Z.$\n\nII. $x\\in Z$ is equivalent to $-x\\in Z.$\n\nIII. $x\\in Z$ if and only if $-x\\in Z.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\in Z$ if and only if $-x\\in Z.$\n\n- $x\\in Z$ is equivalent to $-x\\in Z.$\n\n- If $x\\in Z$ then $-x\\in Z$, and if $-x\\in Z$ then $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f03e2de-9bca-59dd-a15f-41efb9506acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407638} {:answer/id #uuid "2cea398d-083d-5498-8f17-f61d869d92b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407640} {:answer/id #uuid "b4341088-2cf8-57e6-b2a2-ecbb99ffa35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407655} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ae58e7-3641-5e2c-ab97-4e30b902c980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407656} {:answer/id #uuid "fd59c42e-69a0-5435-bc75-63d45cfde2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd59c42e-69a0-5435-bc75-63d45cfde2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407661} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09bd681-f86e-50ed-9622-eb57559db7da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23603376-708c-5de8-b4d0-019ec40ac31f" :question/math-academy-id "q-183340" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** $-2\\in Z,$ **then** $5$ is prime.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If $-2\\notin Z,$ then $5$ is not prime.\n- If $5$ is prime, then $-2\\in Z.$\n- If $5$ is not prime, then $-2\\notin Z.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d3501b-96ef-5fbd-9d93-788021156ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eae7c6d-d67a-5016-94d4-ba2e2583ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407641} {:answer/id #uuid "4dadcbd4-89e1-5f7f-b35e-97b14996f8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407644} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4387d3-9988-5bde-be08-da7a99278325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407647} {:answer/id #uuid "74ea4131-ad8e-5771-b71c-87bc0c921675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407648} {:answer/id #uuid "a2272929-b0b3-5b3c-95b1-8195ac004a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74ea4131-ad8e-5771-b71c-87bc0c921675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407648} :answer-field/id #uuid "99b94ba6-62ef-53e6-b475-846d742173b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c661ac-cdce-5cf9-814b-cfcfe49d2399" :question/math-academy-id "q-82665" :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** you are sick, **then** you are not going to school.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If you are not going to school, then you are sick.\n- If you are not sick, then you are going to school.\n- If you are going to school, then you are not sick." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/0d/0deb19675402bf931111bb80cb616682aac08491b5cd3e1ab5812da05b6df8a8.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive.\n\n- Statement I is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407784} {:answer/id #uuid "6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407789} {:answer/id #uuid "84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407791} {:answer/id #uuid "a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407793} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407801} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" :question/math-academy-id "q-183509" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ $y<y+1⇒{y}^{-1}\\in Q.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$y<y+1$\" is ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{-1}\\in Q$\" is ${Z}^{-}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{Z}^{-}}\\cup {Z}^{-}=∅\\cup {Z}^{-}={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407783} {:answer/id #uuid "339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407785} {:answer/id #uuid "3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407786} {:answer/id #uuid "424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407787} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407803} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216465" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(m):$ $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is ${2k:k\\in Z}.$\n- $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407788} {:answer/id #uuid "7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407790} {:answer/id #uuid "b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407795} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407802} {:answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407804} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" :question/math-academy-id "q-183552" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}\\in N$ and $n+5>1$ \n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{2}\\in N$\" is the set $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+5>1$\" is $N.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=N\\cap N=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfe54f-4c1a-5601-9bd2-0f1892a607c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407860} {:answer/id #uuid "60027804-dbc8-5ba3-b7bd-f44642116bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407866} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1d872d-1050-528d-989c-6e743504ef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407871} {:answer/id #uuid "965d76be-2ba3-58ed-b51e-9ca0ad934ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407872} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5fab01-32db-5160-9eaf-32f4b30665c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60027804-dbc8-5ba3-b7bd-f44642116bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407866} :answer-field/id #uuid "50962eb5-13e0-5527-99de-391f22e7ee03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23e55f80-c083-57f4-820d-7aa2d67256ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105210" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2x+3$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=(x-2)^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3 & =2{x}_{2}+3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(3),$ but $1\\ne 3.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036d9afb-4836-58e3-9109-9dd285d1e857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407857} {:answer/id #uuid "3c173147-60ba-5870-bce4-c2f5db134f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407861} {:answer/id #uuid "4a28a6ba-2618-5929-9239-4e604586dd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407864} {:answer/id #uuid "8a08c9ad-68d3-5048-9aa1-f2e7ffb89cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407868} {:answer/id #uuid "8affdd3e-63cb-5945-abff-fdb11ff377f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a08c9ad-68d3-5048-9aa1-f2e7ffb89cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407868} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e246480-5cbb-5dda-adb1-b0ac60280c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c2e3f55-ea9b-5b9e-badf-26b9e5d93c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-80384" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0a08488ba59344033205903ca973870d0b10837295ced970bf307342e6f7241.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-3,3]\\to [-3,0]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-3)=f(3)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(-3)=f(3)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-3)=f(3)$ but $-3\\ne 3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c583e6-ea60-5d7f-b515-6a1c05522f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407858} {:answer/id #uuid "486699da-5c52-50b7-a5e8-06a33ea08707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407863} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe41323-bdcb-5854-b702-4569e847d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407876} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ef0e88-3d5a-58fe-a094-21c1a2d940b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407877} {:answer/id #uuid "d388ffe7-607b-5621-9d65-a25de45df1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe41323-bdcb-5854-b702-4569e847d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407876} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff19999-9a3e-55e9-8a46-2ed82f902f83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58fa3eda-7303-530c-b266-fd43b4b16a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80370" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c2f22af3b39c28d885ac490aae190e1b2d4ad55828a552fec031069d6d912a2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(0)=f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a59032-d154-5cd1-9f78-1e77115fceb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409347} {:answer/id #uuid "1eba9774-714c-562d-9d13-0447aea71267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409352} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce80dc4-d91c-5754-ac3f-33f63cb1cb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409361} {:answer/id #uuid "789e77ae-772c-5d06-a9df-5fa5f38d83c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409378} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed8879a-6f30-55b0-9f22-b4a53cb96a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789e77ae-772c-5d06-a9df-5fa5f38d83c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409378} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b10380e-960d-5141-bbf4-f7b3eb5af3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ce0c9ed-8f49-5909-b44d-93bd60aa3e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76964" :question/problem "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=9$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1762b1ce-d004-57e6-ae76-4ab71ee3f948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409348} {:answer/id #uuid "96112173-be20-53cc-a11d-2eeab9cf2d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409388} {:answer/id #uuid "a580563f-2be7-54ec-9ef4-eee5fd920596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409394} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d6abbf-0176-582c-8057-a6cee55fd6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409401} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c43ee0-5593-513b-b0c8-b805b7e94411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1762b1ce-d004-57e6-ae76-4ab71ee3f948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409348} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b16f0b8-e8e0-53eb-8868-43987ff6e2b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c433443-9a9c-5d0b-84b5-bc247b100915" :question/math-academy-id "q-103390" :question/problem "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe10586-c932-5ec9-a06b-8cf711a3abc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409353} {:answer/id #uuid "283ae9e3-5cbe-51dc-93f9-66705b94a4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409354} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb7e64-ba7b-569a-a95a-1beb7a13f9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409371} {:answer/id #uuid "62eb87b3-63f2-54d1-be4f-335750bbeb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409373} {:answer/id #uuid "dc534a4c-c7e6-54fe-be3a-e9ddb8d5d053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb7e64-ba7b-569a-a95a-1beb7a13f9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409371} :answer-field/id #uuid "3562348b-86e5-5e46-9766-895a1ac9633f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80a88f89-ac3c-581b-83f4-0bc0a136d398" :question/math-academy-id "q-76972" :question/problem "Given that $8≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=5$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=8-23=-15.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷3=-5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $4∤(-15).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $5∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷5=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e65da69-f3b4-5682-a2d1-fed159b1cca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409346} {:answer/id #uuid "1a91b125-6713-5078-ad8a-07ca9759bd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409349} {:answer/id #uuid "297ef0c5-c965-585b-8851-1eee6d2a0890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409356} {:answer/id #uuid "7457c4d9-6466-5ff1-8ed5-0b76d4baa436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409377} {:answer/id #uuid "94713236-b0f8-5cb1-b15a-fa847167c239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a91b125-6713-5078-ad8a-07ca9759bd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409349} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd48921-20ab-55cd-9e00-cc0f4f65d5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a58d29a-b4cc-50fc-9d69-711422d71682" :question/math-academy-id "q-103490" :question/problem "Given that $13≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=13-(-5)=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $6∣18$ because $18÷6=3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d1a863f-95e0-561d-8b4e-634c0143f09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409367} {:answer/id #uuid "649b2937-4ed2-54a5-868a-a98baf189430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409374} {:answer/id #uuid "656f07f1-9270-5ce4-98e9-a7070185fd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409375} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3b6bc-bfd0-56cc-97c0-969644a1537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409380} {:answer/id #uuid "e330412d-2087-54b1-9337-003e92248756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "656f07f1-9270-5ce4-98e9-a7070185fd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409375} :answer-field/id #uuid "4165585a-237e-5905-a89b-3813ee62ad2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77d765e-3b79-5534-bec0-79f592cc8d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103387" :question/problem "Given that $-3≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-3-15=-18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷2=-9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷6=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷9=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc08d88-96fa-5f9d-8557-7fd8fe262961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409700} {:answer/id #uuid "4f20ef63-3480-55f7-95e5-2cc853da0048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409702} {:answer/id #uuid "573b7e2b-ff52-5029-b107-d0355f51c237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409703} {:answer/id #uuid "846d05a9-d44d-5e50-8190-0ce86e7971a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409705} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dc495a-c9d0-52b6-b7d2-7e013a5139c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f20ef63-3480-55f7-95e5-2cc853da0048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409702} :answer-field/id #uuid "0677d12a-c058-55e0-b0e6-dbd13fd6e32a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c5afa8a-a09e-5d94-9d6f-17bde8db6cd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105422" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 5}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<7}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x<4,\\,{y}^{2}<9}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbcbe48-2124-5037-bac0-c99498ef9fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409698} {:answer/id #uuid "3a63ced9-ca36-5f7e-b7b0-e658c1fa60dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409699} {:answer/id #uuid "408a94f7-529c-5ff0-8b23-26d97a71b99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409701} {:answer/id #uuid "6e671489-f5be-562e-8530-e27db89c1e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409704} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc31443-e5f9-544a-84d7-9325a05e998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbcbe48-2124-5037-bac0-c99498ef9fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409698} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c28df82-226c-551a-9267-de3801bebac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b04f2bd5-de84-5515-a0a5-3d6c81e3d583" :question/math-academy-id "q-161644" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 2-x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\,0<y<1+x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cadc09-da0d-5ae7-aeaf-a7c3e35f4b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409928} {:answer/id #uuid "293c9758-a266-51af-a90b-8e6051ffbc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409944} {:answer/id #uuid "7a906543-e72c-59fe-99da-f316e3cbe719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409960} {:answer/id #uuid "9431a3dc-a0a4-59ec-8755-32a07ab39b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409966} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba1dd0d-6081-556d-8127-a0c2c1fbb715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293c9758-a266-51af-a90b-8e6051ffbc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409944} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1be59e-d97b-50a5-a2c4-37e42fdd9c30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1346b272-ab34-50a3-8e2c-0cbfe3ad7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105231" :question/problem "![](images/37/373448202bfd022f5629219b2c176f4096e4eeae275c1095798d77be92122cc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(1)=4$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $2\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)=2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $4\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440b11b0-4e97-578d-9b81-654a58708367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409949} {:answer/id #uuid "6053e5fd-19ed-51ad-8650-c66168e84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409953} {:answer/id #uuid "79695021-8bd8-583f-b108-d9464cbd796b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409959} {:answer/id #uuid "a629e477-e4cc-52e6-a849-f365e4606d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409976} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb92f17-206c-5a99-bcf5-641db09ec427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6053e5fd-19ed-51ad-8650-c66168e84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409953} :answer-field/id #uuid "468784e2-5fcf-5a84-be1f-9809984d0a37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2021b7c7-5d84-5929-874c-8d6923b11db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-90878" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n\nII. $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f(\\sqrt[3]{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{3}=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={e}^{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g({e}^{y})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y})=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170228c1-67f0-5abd-a1f6-188ba7421a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409936} {:answer/id #uuid "31846889-12fc-5a39-bc5e-44a4d94e96f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409948} {:answer/id #uuid "9b14201a-9ef4-52ea-901b-e2d1a0356b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409971} {:answer/id #uuid "b60f6dc2-6799-5dac-8e8b-7641e9bfdc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409982} {:answer/id #uuid "b805b78c-f5b2-572b-bf64-0e93742027f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b14201a-9ef4-52ea-901b-e2d1a0356b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409971} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae218203-ab98-53f0-9271-2585d0c14ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fb6cc00-0fe3-5564-afaa-6bddf30c9f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90955" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e10e89a-3526-5277-97f0-adb1bf3a9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409932} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8f07e8-6563-560f-83b1-e540eea67b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409938} {:answer/id #uuid "2d675192-6aed-5fb3-b304-c827908d6782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409945} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5d8409-cc3d-50e6-81af-f2299d82d242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409979} {:answer/id #uuid "fae3b726-3cc7-5b0b-9698-60d33fec930f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae3b726-3cc7-5b0b-9698-60d33fec930f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409991} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ac4b53-a5c2-53ff-9394-b86ac9fdb3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" :question/math-academy-id "q-90789" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(2)=4$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e59bd1-b50e-5bf9-aa10-849f69270ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409935} {:answer/id #uuid "4c97f833-9705-5bcc-b81d-efbb617c09be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409950} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff259bb-5279-5455-be8d-39d3935f574d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409956} {:answer/id #uuid "a36d5cc5-d4db-528b-a951-71ab90965a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409974} {:answer/id #uuid "b755a321-e980-5932-8cb8-6008d246a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b755a321-e980-5932-8cb8-6008d246a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409983} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc1ebd2-f871-5635-989f-5d2f5a59d0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d76a2452-4488-5b99-ace5-042b8edc218d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90902" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$\n\nII. $h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$\n\nIII. $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2197db5e-ca7c-51e6-9937-ef34b85e9ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409940} {:answer/id #uuid "78b5fb43-b40d-5220-9328-04422aadc56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409958} {:answer/id #uuid "a770c062-0f3b-5c66-b3cb-db67c9f4a392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409977} {:answer/id #uuid "af41f11c-e537-562e-a402-a550f60bf7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409981} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b3972-fd08-50cd-9235-d2b960271157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b5fb43-b40d-5220-9328-04422aadc56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409958} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f95ef80-066f-5862-85e2-305f98933069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-105246" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(1)=7$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b080a-5175-5b69-aaab-afad022436b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410906} {:answer/id #uuid "3f36077b-cb80-5a78-868e-4f2fe236258b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410909} {:answer/id #uuid "51fd3ae1-bd1b-5102-a9f6-5328bb9d2e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410912} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b77850-35fc-595a-9daf-36d48cabc0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410920} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71c98e-d1d9-58de-93b1-25edf334d81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8b77850-35fc-595a-9daf-36d48cabc0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410920} :answer-field/id #uuid "0baeb570-ecf2-5138-be41-cee0d13033db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00868f73-2a29-5bc5-b5fa-287f2fcfd175" :question/math-academy-id "q-82304" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef3933fc56e981bd0ef6e88754c46f5ab00b15230cce5fc2ea224ecf7c1f8f0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/f0/f020379f306770bd87fc499ac1fef238dc6aa639fb36d5229502fcd7dd455610.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391004d2-2992-548b-b9a0-c0f456c1dbc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410907} {:answer/id #uuid "65c05b37-a9ce-53de-b879-c52f6f237a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410913} {:answer/id #uuid "686b0b91-7c8b-5f63-985c-fe75123e3d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410914} {:answer/id #uuid "d654ebe3-708a-515e-8a05-9d26007e466f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410923} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc1b33a-da6f-5fa1-aa63-322df0372030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecc1b33a-da6f-5fa1-aa63-322df0372030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410925} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94f51c3-8089-500c-bb22-065f6d87882e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f4dc780-0892-5640-8a66-1c76dbdf69ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-170667" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=2x$\n- $h:Z\\to Z$ defined by $h(x)=2x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This means that \n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=4.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 2x & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{2})=2(\\frac{y}{2})=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Notice that if $x$ is an integer number, then $h(x)=2x$ is an even number. This means that the range of $h$ is the set of even numbers.\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in Z$ such that $h(x)=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079dade0-aaba-549e-b166-40f1cd38be43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410904} {:answer/id #uuid "39fe438c-8f49-5303-b841-ca5ec6a58a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410908} {:answer/id #uuid "d70eaee1-5c51-5c83-9b19-ebfef966d252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410924} {:answer/id #uuid "f6346406-ca9d-5e81-813a-255b33f59b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410926} {:answer/id #uuid "fb760290-4b01-5e7a-bcfc-f281f74d98d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb760290-4b01-5e7a-bcfc-f281f74d98d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410927} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3928a6-40a7-5b56-812b-bbcc73b73c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80ecff06-aa66-5d3f-b38e-164bc959ec2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105321" :question/problem "![](images/63/63032bf65ec10d55f9a0b968a7179db934ad6f17b869d50999e4a0f710be9977.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(-1,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/71/71bd13b2dd618fb60cd2e7f6c2e3807f3449e233ccb9b60e1cbd14cc928345b3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116fbc73-ece6-5432-9602-545bf9688e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410905} {:answer/id #uuid "501141cf-63c7-5482-ba0e-93e9b432d4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410910} {:answer/id #uuid "50b734df-b148-59de-b8f1-eb65a75a5857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410911} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6c0231-7d58-5c35-a738-06ae728ba9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410916} {:answer/id #uuid "90d0ac0d-0a31-5b69-a4a2-e449bd7dcdbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6c0231-7d58-5c35-a738-06ae728ba9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410916} :answer-field/id #uuid "26c1f05c-d5ac-55f3-a9f9-404f5c918451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f181e810-d779-5077-8c9b-e3a1b446edcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77480" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:Z\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)={2}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ x+1 & =y \\\\ x & =y-1\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nf(x)=f(y-1)=(y-1)+1=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is both surjective and injective, $f$ is bijective.    \n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=2.$ Then there is no rational number $x$  such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=0.$ Then there is no integer $x$  such that $g(x)=0,$  because ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b4beec-ad53-5851-aa5b-d9750cfb2468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410915} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7110b9-d4b3-5bfe-980b-063cd291d0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410917} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6c406e-0241-5402-873a-5818751247d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410919} {:answer/id #uuid "cf16a0c7-eef9-5edb-86fc-8aac55883795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410921} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdbea3b-43fd-5adf-bc03-99c4bf796e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16a0c7-eef9-5edb-86fc-8aac55883795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410921} :answer-field/id #uuid "9801aa3c-15d6-5b0a-98a6-d929fbad53c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f636c031-3e1e-5fb8-bd29-acadcf4da72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82572" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce68b2f27b88bd43d6eebfa5380a22c00e15e4194e0ad45e6778f761667a3ceb.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x-2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/2f/2ff4c0183b91aa23d780f665aa0f038b374583e9f24e245951c4c69ea5f2e5b8.png)\n\n           \n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=-2\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c2d5e6-6875-5213-b536-37798ae375d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411073} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3a15f1-ad3b-58b1-bc06-3283dca42c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411075} {:answer/id #uuid "6587714c-9457-568c-ac05-8ad90932e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411081} {:answer/id #uuid "8f86c8f5-dc50-5d32-87f9-bb0bad663aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411087} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4a9d6e-1837-5aae-a498-e4bc74af3170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c2d5e6-6875-5213-b536-37798ae375d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411073} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3248ef5-ad73-59c1-88f3-d69d0a99470f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8076c0f-174e-5d1d-bedf-553b0bcc1d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-127969" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nI. $¬Q∧(P∨Q)$\n\nII. $P∧¬Q$\n\nIII. $¬P∧¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,¬Q∧(P∨Q)\\,$ | $\\,P∧¬Q\\,$ | $\\,¬P∧¬Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬Q∧(P∨Q)$ and $P∧¬Q$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273db6e4-b799-50bd-a66c-30f726dc163b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411274} {:answer/id #uuid "2add26dd-e43b-52c7-b2dd-74816713310b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411275} {:answer/id #uuid "4d09cdfe-9545-5f99-9a96-7ea92e64cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411276} {:answer/id #uuid "5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411277} {:answer/id #uuid "a4868990-cd46-582c-81e2-72d1d4d50bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411277} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96c0df0-7687-56d8-909c-da1e58046f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75724" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d4ef47-2283-5013-800d-55532771b590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411315} {:answer/id #uuid "69a086d9-ed8a-5d19-a711-31dea503c803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411324} {:answer/id #uuid "8488ff21-a2ea-5b43-889c-08cc212c8dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411338} {:answer/id #uuid "94a8c259-c943-5b5a-8249-ef29a64409c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411341} {:answer/id #uuid "a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411348} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e8b322-e012-50ae-b918-cd16e0ce7f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213141" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{0,1}}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊃Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0,1}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,{0}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊂{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊃Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊃$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊅$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0,1}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0,1},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊅,$ we have ${0}⊅{0}.$ This is equivalent to ${0}⊄{0},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411289} {:answer/id #uuid "1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the ordered pairs are elements of the relation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411299} {:answer/id #uuid "83250d56-27d9-5d35-998f-7c225e26de93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411336} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ffa5ba-9fc3-5ce6-841a-f2088a84936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411359} {:answer/id #uuid "f5797c74-f7f7-51f3-9dfa-2fc0175bef87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411289} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee0f5da-3466-5854-93e2-01adf980becc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" :question/math-academy-id "q-213003" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{1,0}}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following ordered pairs is an element of the relation \"is **not** a subset of\" on $A?$ \n\n- $({0},{1,0})$\n- $({1,0},{0})$\n- $({1,0},{1,0})$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊈y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $({0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{0})$ is an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊈{0}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411513} {:answer/id #uuid "c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411520} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411524} {:answer/id #uuid "f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} :answer-field/id #uuid "b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29a87319-221e-52be-a49a-7a7f42765bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130357" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam in a class of $25$ students\n- The number of cards drawn until a red card is obtained\n- The number of books published by a publishing house in a month" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c0fe61-4cd5-5060-819b-ff762f83aae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411492} {:answer/id #uuid "500babc9-c423-5d42-a392-144709fe6b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411496} {:answer/id #uuid "5481292b-246a-5d52-93ec-b37d3cc2c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411498} {:answer/id #uuid "e849fced-d809-50ad-a2d4-a3c41ddca395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411531} {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22af52a-6cb4-5c75-ae52-e745c13307b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46c22425-ee4e-5a56-9d8f-8f1ffa9ada86" :question/math-academy-id "q-84748" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} {:answer/id #uuid "4a98ac69-4946-50af-be1a-a87eda63d1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411493} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae6abc-b491-5c69-9fda-9c44145f964f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411497} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3a5183-df01-521a-be7e-92566b8b266a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411528} {:answer/id #uuid "de49179b-7a0c-5bcd-ab74-23a6fdd2bd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b85b9db-4b3a-5931-a044-422d92ddf7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dd54609-ae0c-53a9-a2a1-7823f9aa6595" :question/math-academy-id "q-84747" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of times \"head\" is obtained when a coin is tossed $10$ times\n- The number of students in a class\n- The number of tickets sold for a concert in $3$ days" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of \"heads\" in $10$ tosses can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students in a class cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of tickets sold for a concert cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411476} {:answer/id #uuid "291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411482} {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411512} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" :question/math-academy-id "q-130361" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of trees in a forest of $300$ trees that have bird's nests\n- The number of vaccines administered in a country during one season\n- The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of trees that have bird's nests among $300$ trees can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $300$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although receiving a vaccine can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2993011b-20a1-5970-adb0-721cadaaedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411600} {:answer/id #uuid "4ced8491-b081-5ef8-90f8-3698d8dd8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa79523-2a8b-5ac4-a74a-e388c50d151f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411606} {:answer/id #uuid "7b481987-958d-5896-baf1-928789c0b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411607} {:answer/id #uuid "e7535899-b380-5240-8de6-34d4d073db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ced8491-b081-5ef8-90f8-3698d8dd8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46cc03c-be0d-5f3c-966b-7d8ddd1839d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135308" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26beaa3c-ed64-5711-ae43-5915ddfb5a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411700} {:answer/id #uuid "3400f53e-dd72-52d4-80f7-e75f042482c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411703} {:answer/id #uuid "56148229-ce9a-5da3-a832-6f2fba7b9721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411723} {:answer/id #uuid "7d45cde3-2e6d-51fc-a005-c567ebefb006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411744} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f5746-2e70-560e-b999-9a3896073130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56148229-ce9a-5da3-a832-6f2fba7b9721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411723} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafa25da-d701-5b8d-940d-a4b926749cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d807e12-6960-57f6-b74d-aa3fe66f0f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-178271" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431e7942-0839-55b7-a404-74d95db870c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411981} {:answer/id #uuid "4687ae48-3f94-52e2-9dea-bc272c1082bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411982} {:answer/id #uuid "5d69edd9-6b87-5ce4-a237-f6f2f7e1d07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411985} {:answer/id #uuid "cde51bb4-f6f6-5104-86ea-3da2a88dc7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411994} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eb5ef4-1688-5731-842c-a125cdee48be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde51bb4-f6f6-5104-86ea-3da2a88dc7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411994} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f36666-4b80-55b9-8c5c-1ce4a6ba661d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dd9397-7325-5b4d-9df5-fd6f72af0a3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76978" :question/problem "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=7$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c37123-a096-5bf6-b2a7-5752505e1833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411976} {:answer/id #uuid "26490cf8-9eff-5b3a-b0d3-8c64c57d3157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411978} {:answer/id #uuid "2c364ca7-bc65-5525-9cbb-b33295254414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411979} {:answer/id #uuid "5825c4b0-9c95-5b01-bd68-b59a82484df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411983} {:answer/id #uuid "c31f3f15-6b9f-50fc-afb7-ae7ee0f2806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26490cf8-9eff-5b3a-b0d3-8c64c57d3157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411978} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfebcc4f-6a0a-526a-a3ca-b56faab77eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e4ac9e6-575d-537d-b731-f3556414b8f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103492" :question/problem "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=6$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07424979-9774-53c4-849f-0c08fd54fc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411974} {:answer/id #uuid "08bd5615-539e-5361-90d4-ed7d60cee26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411975} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc657a-27b5-5e42-929a-143a6d8eadb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411987} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba8fc9a-b908-579d-8f19-6c7445fb911f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411989} {:answer/id #uuid "eb35e354-c87a-5af4-b949-2eb9feda22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08bd5615-539e-5361-90d4-ed7d60cee26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411975} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a66d7e4-64aa-595c-8772-a5f8626e54fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd2ee89b-0850-5297-a810-7e54facce40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212522" :question/problem "Given that $-7≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=3$\n- $n=7$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-7-(-3)=-4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-4)$ because $(-4)÷2=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176604ed-c837-5d39-bf7d-e8e2128262e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412055} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1ed2f9-1e46-534a-a381-abb6e053ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412056} {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd590b8-7f9d-5cdd-9e3e-23772a325ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412058} {:answer/id #uuid "e04a3a05-fb55-5020-b5ef-f2fc7abaa120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412057} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42481" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$\n- $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c08a4-3464-5326-833d-0cac32b879f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412794} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfbf6a0-2987-5862-8e6d-5405fa26152f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412795} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9aacf5-3935-547f-8a01-96b4e4f8e273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412797} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412799} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1cc24-066e-5fd9-aec3-63dcebd27b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412799} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7e02fc-389b-5a53-ab92-9e7d664ea877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71a6807f-51a3-5e94-a65a-0087aa5dc119" :question/math-academy-id "q-330333" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d3215-6073-5918-a9fe-1dcc32903f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412793} {:answer/id #uuid "b96cdf9a-49a4-54e5-9dfc-848510bf8222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412796} {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412798} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c4b1ce-27b0-544b-a5c2-9c96a3531721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412800} {:answer/id #uuid "eae566d9-a61e-5385-975a-ff789b6dc62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f03c56a-d1c9-5e79-89bf-0c51f2598a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a0af42-71ea-5bb3-8eb8-b8b29ef8a2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330354" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d2f207a-8e99-5520-ab9f-266c8f54465b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to a Residue Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae50f18-3a44-5059-859d-62a308e0626c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412825} {:answer/id #uuid "536a2ad9-31ea-58bb-ad50-9a311d120bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e813eca-88bc-51a0-a774-be6fb12da67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412854} {:answer/id #uuid "a3be6687-8c01-58a1-8b6f-55c990a13f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412883} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf198f1-51ce-5197-901f-4a57ec6ca534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3be6687-8c01-58a1-8b6f-55c990a13f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412883} :answer-field/id #uuid "86eeeda2-7dc4-5dc4-b9bd-b1e07b0c7e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e36839-2675-57c4-abf9-91a0b0717003" :question/math-academy-id "q-214450" :question/problem "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[4]_{7}?$\n\nI. $-3$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $18$" :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a9a1c5c-d379-566e-88ec-4f742d23a956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412830} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdd9f0e-932d-558a-9378-6f478cae4f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412831} {:answer/id #uuid "d3abf09a-f4e8-5b21-bd9a-3231221ada27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412910} {:answer/id #uuid "d5516742-1e80-5031-a6c5-e5f0e763ef73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412913} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8754b8-9881-5ed1-bb9f-dc739076a719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5516742-1e80-5031-a6c5-e5f0e763ef73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412913} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c42e18f-3211-5db3-867f-3fb18fe023c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476816fb-a049-55c8-86b3-04683cf8a95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78604" :question/problem "Which of the following residue classes modulo $6$ contain the integer $7?$\n\nI. $[-5]_{6}$\n\nII. $[3]_{6}$\n\nIII. $[1]_{6}$" :question/worked-solution "We have $7\\in [a]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\n7≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $7\\in [-5]_{6}$ because $7≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $7\\notin [3]_{6}$ because $7≢3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $7\\in [1]_{6}$ because $7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141ec379-3ffa-52e6-bad6-ca1691d2141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412818} {:answer/id #uuid "575da76a-a021-573e-9647-5cecb1252317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412844} {:answer/id #uuid "90050eab-d210-5e6f-ab25-dd473065930c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III 