${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1643f0dc-f7e7-5dad-a5b8-aae4b765e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414760} {:answer/id #uuid "68101330-8a2c-5b91-a587-3cdb07c9e267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414767} {:answer/id #uuid "945c7fb5-ffad-5ef7-8bdb-b1a3bf71f097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414771} {:answer/id #uuid "c1849629-2bb6-5ec1-9c2e-547f226eb5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414775} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbf4f19-642b-5af3-8c6c-71292db7385f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1643f0dc-f7e7-5dad-a5b8-aae4b765e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414760} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff707b18-5e56-5002-82ef-862a25939ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7554a362-8a42-5a06-a982-68a08488fe59" :question/math-academy-id "q-125687" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1ba55883d76b7e48148febcf6203a987575e78179c2fcde1963ab9db34a37738.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e7/e783fb958f01da9552ef4258adf4bed1f7b083520ad9a59082b5479dc2fc7d40.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/20/20798c6992f536d4f280243746f8599bd4589bd69e253caeed65150b8a9944b7.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab1a91d-d33a-5e51-b220-e0e4da05d420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414931} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414985} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d03008-076d-5979-8ec2-d15997b802f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414986} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51c39b-f541-5db9-9b1d-bc0125830007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415009} {:answer/id #uuid "f95294ea-d39c-5f5e-8043-8a30f497210f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414985} :answer-field/id #uuid "185bfed6-c6e9-5751-adee-92e27148dc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdd37720-ffc4-552d-8b81-f3aa2f866efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103026" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\nI. $k=8$\n\nII. $k=-12$\n\nIII. $k=-15$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=8.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,8)=8\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-12,8)=4\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=-15,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-15,8)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-15.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32cab0-a947-5df3-b52a-1e20d4a89706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414933} {:answer/id #uuid "19a4b986-70e3-5216-b16f-03a5fda6657d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414939} {:answer/id #uuid "5a04721a-ae8a-5b6e-8878-aabea63fdaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414956} {:answer/id #uuid "636fa707-191d-5267-8fee-440385919afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414961} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414987} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b518f5-1d9e-5140-a83d-d1e076453bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eaf9f361-8af6-5a67-abf5-86a0bbac7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103018" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\nI. $k=12$\n\nII. $k=3$\n\nIII. $k=-5$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=10.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,10)=2\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=12.$\n\n- If $k=3,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,10)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=3.$\n\n- If $k=-5,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-5,10)=5\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415150} {:answer/id #uuid "75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415151} {:answer/id #uuid "87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415152} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415153} {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415154} :answer-field/id #uuid "288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5642c9-3238-501d-b001-abe804dbf74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415164} {:answer/id #uuid "42236876-92a9-57f6-83c9-30fca5f7268d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415169} {:answer/id #uuid "49d6bd5f-dc44-5b25-a899-60660d018a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415170} {:answer/id #uuid "c9063210-0eaf-5f51-9f26-d06d56f1944e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415178} {:answer/id #uuid "ead9dfa3-e4dc-59d6-a1e9-1696a9df2aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f5642c9-3238-501d-b001-abe804dbf74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415164} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e45ba6-a42f-5129-95cb-6948d10b75e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d6a13d3-ad69-5578-b6a3-a3b3842749f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-183354" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\ge 2.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\le -2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ or if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ if and only if $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415283} {:answer/id #uuid "587cc8ba-51a2-5f70-b7ab-e29504848366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415289} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f13577-89cf-5b55-b7e4-ab887172e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415296} {:answer/id #uuid "dad516a1-d6d5-51c7-9929-12667a17dd33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415300} {:answer/id #uuid "fe81a0b5-f0a2-514d-9d40-4d11839b169e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415283} :answer-field/id #uuid "01518b07-f415-5bbf-8b57-bf0d859e6135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8f010b-1d85-5f55-981d-5803f1cc03c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125645" :question/problem "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6bbb37-e5ee-59d8-9780-e4f18b4fd703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415286} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf5c5a-bb1b-59ca-aa2f-2855f76bd340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415287} {:answer/id #uuid "5d659d54-987d-5ce7-9597-87369b068528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415290} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415293} {:answer/id #uuid "e36e6492-906e-584d-9d76-b86aee6f54cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415293} :answer-field/id #uuid "340ffb7b-df1a-5614-b77d-8024725af8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77e87c43-f6db-5082-9277-93ef32960dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-125659" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2y\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4x}{1+4{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143f48c0-a939-5a4c-b987-afdbbefec79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415447} {:answer/id #uuid "22066df8-6293-5194-a873-db4b694c6674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415450} {:answer/id #uuid "8e60b08a-cd17-575f-8815-735e23870208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415460} {:answer/id #uuid "b446fc5b-8c89-5d67-acf5-bbcf1be8249d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415462} {:answer/id #uuid "fe594f39-ea49-5697-a493-c1ebeaf625b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22066df8-6293-5194-a873-db4b694c6674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415450} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a7cadd-9704-50b9-80ee-df6bc66a727b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d883981-f066-5218-a1bf-dd76574de46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42469" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415595} {:answer/id #uuid "b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415596} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415597} {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415600} {:answer/id #uuid "d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415600} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213405" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36?$\n\n- $5$\n- $13$\n- $24$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $5$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(5,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $13$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(13,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(24,36)=12\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415586} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415587} {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415592} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415598} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213407" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27?$\n\n- $11$\n- $16$\n- $23$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $11$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(11,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $16$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(16,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $23$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(23,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415996} {:answer/id #uuid "6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416014} {:answer/id #uuid "781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416015} {:answer/id #uuid "e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416036} {:answer/id #uuid "fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415996} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213261" :question/problem "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{2,5,8\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(2,8)$\n\nIII. $(8,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415994} {:answer/id #uuid "4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416006} {:answer/id #uuid "cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416030} {:answer/id #uuid "e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416034} {:answer/id #uuid "e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415994} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/math-academy-id "q-213262" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415992} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415997} {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416002} {:answer/id #uuid "6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416011} {:answer/id #uuid "edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416002} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" :question/math-academy-id "q-213167" :question/problem "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X=\\{2,4,6\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(4,4)$\n\nIII. $(6,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "306be17f-f950-57e2-9cb7-2b33c8a4929e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab03ed4-fb9b-5074-9880-fe1c53b1877b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416708} {:answer/id #uuid "2e52c3bd-2b7a-5a24-95b6-a56a7a15447c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416713} {:answer/id #uuid "b4de6a26-5382-5b6d-bf0e-07b0e5da5b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416780} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c5b715-7c1c-5058-8ab1-617e85ee6cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416795} {:answer/id #uuid "f7295677-3fca-59ea-b647-9db4f20d343a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4de6a26-5382-5b6d-bf0e-07b0e5da5b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416780} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7897328-67d4-5121-9241-5f901a3d93b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653fba49-a6b9-5e57-b514-cf99f2424cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213246" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡2y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y≡x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,1)\\notin R$ since $1≢2(1)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ while\n\n- $(7,7)\\in R$ since $7≡2(7)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\notin T$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≢x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "306be17f-f950-57e2-9cb7-2b33c8a4929e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bdce35-1a6d-5b3f-a49e-4f39f1e7cd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416711} {:answer/id #uuid "58ae1950-b540-541c-bb35-734835ed6837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416731} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2cab9d-46e7-5fca-9e2e-839c1847e800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416750} {:answer/id #uuid "a67c2cc7-31d2-5884-b5e7-bd42e8198442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416774} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d8d161-c088-50a0-8cdb-b2dfab2b3cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21bdce35-1a6d-5b3f-a49e-4f39f1e7cd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416711} :answer-field/id #uuid "a904c1ac-f48c-541f-87b2-64440b6f4699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7095468b-6aec-5ce6-83d1-ea12403a6b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213240" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x+y)∣10,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $4∣({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y+1),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin R$ since $2+2=4$ and $4∤10,$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in R$ since $1+1=2$ and $2∣10.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣({x}^{2}-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin T$ since $2∤(2+1),$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in T$ since $1∣(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003f486a-2600-584c-92ff-478adde52a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416691} {:answer/id #uuid "4c56e2bb-43ed-5906-85e4-b291c9d37ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416727} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2585bc-b01e-5f72-8bd0-21e0708314ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416764} {:answer/id #uuid "a753fcea-864c-5d1d-9515-36b40da1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416775} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8cdb8-d4be-5768-99b5-0263aa670d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a753fcea-864c-5d1d-9515-36b40da1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416775} :answer-field/id #uuid "50c36a75-cd57-5a00-815a-7d34757ac50b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a27c07-b36b-5525-ba6b-847b13ebf516" :question/math-academy-id "q-213228" :question/problem "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} {:answer/id #uuid "7e200172-34ca-5ab5-a0aa-e59cf6e4d94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416884} {:answer/id #uuid "8162f83a-7db2-5f3b-82a9-fd24f5cd3429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416886} {:answer/id #uuid "9432a7e4-d864-5909-96fb-bc5b42aaf049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416891} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f74fae-3121-59b5-8358-f435d8ddb989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139820" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n\nII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n\nIII. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e7de96-4120-5130-bf63-a0d6c2259820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416885} {:answer/id #uuid "85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416889} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0857c3-5c1b-52e2-a099-8d64c550ebdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416900} {:answer/id #uuid "daab6621-7a59-5df9-94ae-46892b25c2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416905} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f31955-0b4d-58d4-8a01-1883943c46ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416889} :answer-field/id #uuid "3688953c-5564-5430-b587-22b1cb1cae35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143552" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\mu \\cdot \\sigma -\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\mu -\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\sigma -max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{15})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expressions I and II are *not* statistics. They are functions that contain the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416867} {:answer/id #uuid "312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416870} {:answer/id #uuid "363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416871} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa121b-5ca3-59a5-9ca2-3cfda696c7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416907} {:answer/id #uuid "ea421d4e-7720-57f3-94e7-9b66a798936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416867} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48c9bdb-925a-55e9-8810-2371ff4500d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" :question/math-academy-id "q-139813" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions I and II are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111f2a1e-49a4-5425-9c30-77e8f10a7813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416866} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0c5de3-7c79-5b7e-a720-d262f7cd52f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416875} {:answer/id #uuid "4e974b83-28b6-5e89-995d-50a977968dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416877} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbcabc8-67e3-58c7-ada3-e4560b3ebf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416878} {:answer/id #uuid "7a32c857-a580-5328-90f1-f1b03c19902c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e974b83-28b6-5e89-995d-50a977968dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416877} :answer-field/id #uuid "0717ebb9-5fd9-5b4c-acc5-d9a8ab6e92d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b59e493b-eefe-51a6-8a6a-b1643795634d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139806" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}$\n- $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n- $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416864} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416869} {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416902} {:answer/id #uuid "e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "352a7d2c-4aba-5246-8a20-ed79de007b95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Matrix Expression Involving the Hadamard Product is Well-Defined"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "368e5c38-687c-5476-a766-53fe59679efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416956} {:answer/id #uuid "a2de33de-7c02-5cb9-b000-8fd4ffa0668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416962} {:answer/id #uuid "a81f915c-2e4f-5036-ade2-5fb2255941e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416963} {:answer/id #uuid "a859c734-1278-56c3-b313-37ad8c178a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416964} {:answer/id #uuid "cee12af1-4ad7-5e93-a79a-1c4026db489e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "368e5c38-687c-5476-a766-53fe59679efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416956} :answer-field/id #uuid "f680b101-94a2-558f-9708-756d2ba22da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6deba07-cdc0-5243-9183-e0064a49692b" :question/math-academy-id "q-274938" :question/problem "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n\n- $YX⊙Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$\n- $X⊙Yu$\n- $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)⊙YX{X}^{T}$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nFirst, notice that $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $u$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector.\n\nNow, let's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $YX$ are $2\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $X$ are $3\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Yu$ are $2\\times 1.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $YX{X}^{T}$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417360} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417361} {:answer/id #uuid "41d70487-8623-57c9-b21d-be91769cf161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417366} {:answer/id #uuid "5daf2709-08dc-5216-87eb-2c674ea61e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417368} {:answer/id #uuid "932f9073-db7f-59db-843a-495c4d7ae846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417360} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d95426-738e-5629-870c-65da360e7800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75505" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dee3e5-8fb2-530f-bfa9-3599d563e0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417398} {:answer/id #uuid "2e24d934-08c4-58e3-be52-ce9f5121ebde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417408} {:answer/id #uuid "653569ac-ed4a-561b-b9be-9d858125d297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417436} {:answer/id #uuid "ead62325-324d-5dce-b93f-38dca6a0bf33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417531} {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7a1b4f-e571-5ec0-a3c9-9dbfbbd624fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213599" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=0,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c5373d-0629-56d7-901e-7af879dd9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417386} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417392} {:answer/id #uuid "32e8c8fd-be74-587d-a3b4-8214f956aaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417412} {:answer/id #uuid "8281f135-368b-5bd3-8ea5-ebad2352c486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417463} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7470ff-d87b-54d1-8fb8-68a8dbdf78fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417392} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
